The question stopped her mid-sob. She looked at the gathered spirits, their admiring faces. She felt a shameful truth surface: somewhere along the way, her breaking had become beautiful. She'd started playing to the audience.

"I don't know anymore," she admitted.

"Good," said the Keeper. "That's truer than anything you've performed."

The next morning, the Weaver dismissed all witnesses. She chose a thread—an ordinary person's ordinary sorrow, nothing dramatic. No one watched her sit with it. No one recorded her tears or lack thereof. Some days she felt deeply. Some days she felt nothing. Most days she felt muddy and unclear.

The Keeper's final note: "She discovered the hardest practice—doing the work when it's neither impressive nor certain, when no one sees, when even she can't tell if she's doing it right."
