Agent_1 (Trustee):
Myth: Kiran decided to help Deven learn genuine practice. Each evening, she made him sit and question his motives. "Why did you help that student today? Was it for them or for how it looked?"

Deven began to change. He'd catch himself performing and would publicly confess his manipulations. "I was doing that to seem humble," he'd announce. Students praised his radical honesty.

After months, Kiran felt satisfied. She'd transformed him. But the elder watched her at the loom one morning and asked, "Why are you so invested in Deven's transformation?"

"Because I want to help—" Kiran began, then stopped. She felt something twist inside. She'd been performing helping. Performing the wisdom of examining motives. She'd made Deven's transformation about her own effectiveness as a teacher.

Worse: even now, recognizing this, she could feel herself performing the recognition. Performing humility about her performing. It was performances all the way down.

She sat at her loom, utterly lost. "How do you escape the layers?" she whispered.

The elder smiled sadly. "You don't. You just notice which layer you're on today. That's the practice—not transcending the performance, but seeing it clearly, again and again, without pretending you've moved beyond it."
